00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:48.820
That will do, Miss Adamson.

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.280
Well, I'm not, and Dr. Fleming's...

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:50.960
Miss Adamson!

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:52.540
Oh, for heaven's sake, let her know.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:56.800
We've isolated an enzyme which regenerates cells.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:15:59.960
It was the summer of 1961 that we were shooting it.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:11.310
And it was supposed to be the summer of 1970, the far, far future as we were concerned at that time, trying to imagine what it would be like.

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:20.010
And I came up, this is another, I sound as if I'm playing my own trumpet, but the only two ideas I had, apart from telling them to get Julie Christie.

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:27.750
The other idea I had was, I think there will be more women in responsible positions in public life.

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:29.980
because originally the...

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:38.620
doctor who created Andromeda was a bloke called George Dornay. And I said, well, who

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:42.600
can we have in it that would be a woman? I said, well, how about this biologist? And

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:52.180
they said, yeah, great. Why not? So she was rechristened Madeline Dornay and Mary Morris

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:57.100
kindly consented to perform it excellently. There was a bit of a glitch because the script

00:16:57.100 --> 00:16:59.920
had to be written. All they did was cross out George.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.490
and put in Madeleine, but they left in a bit where it said,

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:09.740
Madeleine puts a hand round Andromeda's shoulder,

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:13.890
removes it, stuffs pipe and lights it,

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:16.410
which Mary didn't actually do.

00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:18.690
I thought that was rather a shame.

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:23.210
Interestingly enough, Mary Morris was a great sculptress, wasn't she?

00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:23.470
Yes.

00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:25.210
Always making things out of wood.

00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:28.050
She'd built this house in Chalet in Switzerland, do you remember?

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:28.950
Yes, yes.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.680
Oh, yes, that's right, she did.

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:37.220
Yes, she had an old chalet, which she restored by hand herself.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:38.840
She lived there with Mary Ellis, is that correct?

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:39.700
Mary Ellis, yes, that's right.

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:42.580
Because it was quite an amazing thing, really,

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:46.280
because Mary Ellis was a great one for the men.

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:50.250
And suddenly, you know, she fell for Mary Morris.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:52.200
I remember my mum saying,

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:55.100
now then, Susan, you're working with somebody

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:57.960
who we know prefers other people, you know.

00:17:58.160 --> 00:17:59.780
I don't want to go into it too much, she said.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.590
And let me just say this.

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:04.100
If we ever get kissed by a woman, she said,

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:07.050
just remember that you won't want to go back to a man.

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:09.240
I thought, how did my mum think that?

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:11.280
